WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.080
I'm Dr. Andy Galpin,

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.610
I'm a professor of kinesiology

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:09.000
in the Center for Sport
Performance at Cal State Fullerton.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:09.840
In that center,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.600
our mission is to conduct
and disseminate research

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.880
that enhances human performance.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.220
In addition to my role
as a scientist there,

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.390
I've also worked directly

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:21.000
with professional athletes
for nearly 20 years,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.060
I've been fortunate to work
with a number of high profile

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.950
and highly successful individuals.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.160
This would include: Olympic
gold and silver medalists,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.200
world champions, MVPs,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.540
Cy Young Winners, Hall of Famers,

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.210
and more, across a variety of sports.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.130
But not only have I had a passion

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.200
for conducting human performance research

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.963
and applying it to real
athletes in the field,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.440
I've also made a career emphasis

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.050
to get that information out to the public.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:50.760
So in an effort to continue that,

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.890
and at the strong encouragement of Andrew

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.270
as well as the rest of the team here

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.090
at the Huberman Lab
podcast and many others,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.150
I'm extremely excited to
be launching my own show.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.000
My main focus of this show
is not necessarily physiology

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.460
that is healthy, or really healthy.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:06.930
It's what is maximally healthy.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.900
How do I optimize and peak
my physical performance?

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.910
Now, I'm fully aware
most of you listening,

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.340
and in fact, honestly myself as well,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.620
have really no interest
in breaking world records

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.580
in any physical feats, now
or anytime in the future.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.087
And so you may be asking yourself,

00:01:22.087 --> 00:01:24.720
"Why would this information matter to me?"

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.100
Well think about it this way.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:30.000
If we understand the
tools and tactics required

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.280
to perform at this kind of a level,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.430
when dialed up to 10 in the
most extreme environments,

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.400
that's going to help us
understand what to do

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.440
when the dial's only at five or six.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.530
Along the way, I'm going to
share with you all of my secrets.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.420
Well, maybe not all of them.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.350
But the things that we use

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:47.970
that you're not going to necessarily find

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.350
in the peer-reviewed research.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.170
While doing that, I'm going to
be sharing with you candidly

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.840
some advanced tools,
technologies, and tactics

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.330
that may not be within your budget,

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.690
or even frankly available
to the general public.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.760
So at the same time, I
will go out of my way

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.100
to ensure I mention alternatives,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.060
so what strategies are available,
ideally low or free cost,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.890
that are still scientifically valid ways

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:14.700
to either identify,
interpret, or intervene,

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.710
and drive the appropriate
physiological change

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:17.820
that you're looking for.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.370
So admittedly, while we're
going to be sticking mostly

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.000
to the peer-reviewed science,
I will from time to time

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.820
enter in my personal opinion
from my coaching practice,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.680
and I will be sure I'm clarifying you

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.120
when it is my opinion,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.280
versus what is the scientific consensus.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.820
With that all being said, I'm
incredibly excited and proud

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.920
to release to you this first season.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.090
I want to keep the content as
high of quality as possible.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.800
So this first season is going to only be

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.050
about a dozen or so episodes.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.460
After that, we're going to take some time

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:50.610
to go back to the lab,
work on some more stuff,

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.290
and only come back for season two

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.960
once we've achieved that
same level of excellence

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.270
we hope we've accomplished in season one.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:00.330
I sincerely thank you for your
time and attention thus far,

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.793
and hope to see you back
here for episode one.

